#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# piecewise

#% text
# `piecewise([[v1, c1], [v2, c2], ...])` gives the value `vi` of the first case whose condition `ci` is true, and 0 when none is.

#% text
# `piecewise(cases, default)` gives `default` when no condition is true.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The conditions are tried in order; later cases may assume the earlier conditions were false.

#% item
# When a condition cannot be decided (it involves a symbol), the call stays, so it can be plotted, integrated or differentiated as a function of that symbol.

#% item
# Inside a function, `if ... then ... else` is often simpler; `piecewise` is a value that symbolic functions understand.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
f = piecewise([[x^2, x < 0], [x, x < 1]], 1)
f /. x -> -2
#% output
#= 4

#% cell
f /. x -> 1/2
#% output
#= 1/2

#% cell
f /. x -> 5
#% output
#= 1

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Without a default, 0:

#% cell
piecewise([[1, 3 < 2]])
#% output
#= 0

#% text
# With a symbol, the call stays:

#% cell
f
#% output
#= piecewise([[x^2, x < 0], [x, x < 1]], 1)

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# A tent function, plotted:

#% cell
tent = piecewise([[1 + t, t < 0], [1 - t, t < 1]], 0)
plot(tent, [t, -2, 2])
#% output
#= image("piecewise.files/piecewise-6.svgz")

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The same choice written with `if`, for numbers:

#% cell
fn g(x) = if x < 0 then x^2 else if x < 1 then x else 1
[g(-2), g(1/2), g(5)]
#% output
#= [4, 1/2, 1]

#% text
# `unit_step` is a two-case piecewise function:

#% cell
[unit_step(-1), unit_step(2)]
#% output
#= [0, 1]

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# The derivative of a piecewise function is worked out piece by piece; it holds away from the breakpoints, where the
# function may not be differentiable:

#% cell
d(tent, t)
#% output
#= piecewise([[1, t < 0], [-1, t < 1]], 0)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `unit_step`, `clamp`, `abs`, `plot`, `d`
